<p data-narration-id="n18">For “Give Two Things” questions (2 marks), keep your answers short and specific. Each inference must be supported by a detail from the source or your own knowledge. For example: “I can infer that the Soviet Union feared losing control of Eastern Europe. I know this because the Brezhnev Doctrine stated the USSR had the right to intervene in any socialist country where communism was threatened.” Don’t write too much — two clear points with supporting detail is all that’s needed.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">For “Explain One Cause” questions (4 marks), make sure you identify the cause clearly in your first sentence, then develop it with three further linked points. Use connectives like “this was because,” “which meant that,” and “as a result” to show a chain of reasoning. For example: “One cause of détente was the cost of the arms race. This was because both superpowers were spending huge sums on nuclear weapons, which diverted resources from their economies. As a result, both sides had a financial incentive to agree limits on nuclear weapons, leading to SALT 1 in 1972.”</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">For “How Far Do You Agree” questions (16 marks) on the end of the Cold War, examiners want you to analyse <em>multiple factors</em> and reach a <em>judgement</em>. Don’t just list factors — compare them. For example: “While Gorbachev’s reforms were important, they would not have succeeded without Reagan’s economic pressure, which forced the USSR to the negotiating table. However, the fundamental weakness of the Soviet economy was arguably the root cause, as it made reform necessary in the first place.” A clear, reasoned conclusion will push you into the top level.</p>

