+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For “Give Two Things” questions (2 marks), keep your answers short and specific. Each inference must be supported by a detail from the source or your own knowledge. For example: “I can infer that the Soviet Union feared losing control of Eastern Europe. I know this because the Brezhnev Doctrine stated the USSR had the right to intervene in any socialist country where communism was threatened.” Don’t write too much — two clear points with supporting detail is all that’s needed.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For a 4-mark consequence question, identify the consequence, then explain how the event led to it. Use precise knowledge and link cause to effect. For example, explain how the invasion damaged Soviet influence among Western communist parties.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F4: Replace the incorrect period-study question format with the verified 4-mark task type."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 6,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">For “Explain One Cause” questions (4 marks), make sure you identify the cause clearly in your first sentence, then develop it with three further linked points. Use connectives like “this was because,” “which meant that,” and “as a result” to show a chain of reasoning. For example: “One cause of détente was the cost of the arms race. This was because both superpowers were spending huge sums on nuclear weapons, which diverted resources from their economies. As a result, both sides had a financial incentive to agree limits on nuclear weapons, leading to SALT 1 in 1972.”</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">For a 4-mark consequence question, identify the consequence clearly, then explain how the event led to it. Use connectives such as &ldquo;this meant that&rdquo; and &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; to show a chain of reasoning. For example, explain how the human-rights commitments at Helsinki gave Eastern European dissidents a way to challenge their governments.</p>",
